1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:08,000
It's civilization's pipe dream,

2
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:16,000
surging with life-giving water and tons of waste,

3
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:21,000
feeding the fixtures that spoil us and flushing us with pride.

4
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:24,000
Its potential is explosive, and its history overflows

5
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:25,000
with innovation.

6
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:29,000
Now, plumbing on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
If there's one fact of life we all hold in common, it's our

8
00:00:47,080 --> 00:00:50,760
dependence on water. Regardless of race, creed,

9
00:00:50,761 --> 00:00:54,320
or social status, we all rely on it for our very survival.

10
00:00:54,321 --> 00:00:59,060
We've been depending on the craft and science of plumbing

11
00:00:59,160 --> 00:01:03,060
for thousands of years, as it enables us to bring fresh water

12
00:01:03,160 --> 00:01:07,200
where we need it and take dangerous wastewater away.

13
00:01:07,300 --> 00:01:11,200
Even though we kind of become complacent in the industry

14
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:12,900
in so much as what we do every day,

15
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:14,340
we kind of lose sight of the fact

16
00:01:14,440 --> 00:01:16,580
that the plumber is a very important part

17
00:01:16,680 --> 00:01:18,180
in the health industry.

18
00:01:18,280 --> 00:01:20,420
We don't have the outbreaks of diseases.

19
00:01:20,520 --> 00:01:24,320
We don't have any of the things that we had as little as a home

20
00:01:24,321 --> 00:01:27,060
years ago or less.

21
00:01:27,160 --> 00:01:30,020
Throughout history, the ability to control water

22
00:01:30,120 --> 00:01:31,860
has been one of the defining elements

23
00:01:31,960 --> 00:01:34,760
of a sophisticated culture.

24
00:01:34,860 --> 00:01:36,760
Plumbing is civilization.

25
00:01:36,860 --> 00:01:40,560
Just about every civilization that we look back on in antiquity

26
00:01:40,660 --> 00:01:43,880
that we study and admire for their contributions

27
00:01:43,980 --> 00:01:48,080
to art and literature and science, technology, civil

28
00:01:48,180 --> 00:01:53,580
order also were highly developed plumbers.

29
00:01:53,581 --> 00:01:56,920
Early concepts of plumbing, mere ditches in the ground,

30
00:01:57,020 --> 00:01:59,280
have developed into complex networks of pipes

31
00:01:59,380 --> 00:02:01,820
capable of supplying water to cities of millions.

32
00:02:05,460 --> 00:02:09,320
Ever wondered how much plumbing pipe you've got in your home?

33
00:02:09,420 --> 00:02:12,260
The average American house has over 700 feet,

34
00:02:12,360 --> 00:02:15,240
hidden between the walls and under the floorboards,

35
00:02:15,340 --> 00:02:18,340
allowing everyone in the house to consume more than 50 gallons

36
00:02:18,440 --> 00:02:20,700
of water per day.

37
00:02:20,800 --> 00:02:22,000
Whenever somebody thinks of a plumber,

38
00:02:22,001 --> 00:02:24,160
they think of the guy that came and fixed their toilet

39
00:02:24,260 --> 00:02:25,440
and charged too much.

40
00:02:25,540 --> 00:02:28,500
But it's quite a mechanical trade with a great deal of skill

41
00:02:28,600 --> 00:02:31,540
and a great deal of knowledge involved in it.

42
00:02:31,640 --> 00:02:34,380
To reach the fixtures that place it at our fingertips,

43
00:02:34,480 --> 00:02:36,540
water is often transported hundreds of miles

44
00:02:36,640 --> 00:02:39,980
from its source, above and below ground.

45
00:02:42,960 --> 00:02:45,160
Once it reaches the city, thousands

46
00:02:45,260 --> 00:02:46,760
of miles of underground water mains

47
00:02:46,860 --> 00:02:51,300
carry it into buildings, where water flows hot and cold

48
00:02:51,301 --> 00:02:54,740
at our beck and call.

49
00:02:54,840 --> 00:02:58,300
One universal force of nature drives the water from point A

50
00:02:58,400 --> 00:03:02,440
to point B and maintains pressure in the pipes.

51
00:03:02,540 --> 00:03:05,040
Gravity.

52
00:03:05,140 --> 00:03:07,880
Water is pumped to the top of giant towers, which

53
00:03:07,980 --> 00:03:12,160
are connected to the mains beneath the streets.

54
00:03:12,260 --> 00:03:14,560
As long as the tower is higher than the water's final

55
00:03:14,660 --> 00:03:18,300
destination, gravity will maintain pressure and force

56
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:21,300
water through the pipes.

57
00:03:21,301 --> 00:03:24,060
In buildings that are taller than the water tower,

58
00:03:24,061 --> 00:03:27,300
water is pumped to a series of pressure tanks inside,

59
00:03:27,301 --> 00:03:30,300
each using gravity to feed the floors below it.

60
00:03:32,300 --> 00:03:36,300
After water is used, gravity once again carries it away.

61
00:03:36,301 --> 00:03:40,300
Sewer pipes are built at an angle so that wastewater

62
00:03:40,301 --> 00:03:43,300
can flow downhill.

63
00:03:43,301 --> 00:03:46,300
After sewage has been treated, it flows by gravity

64
00:03:46,301 --> 00:03:50,300
into the nearest waterway, where it's reclaimed by the Earth.

65
00:03:50,301 --> 00:03:55,300
Mastering the basics of plumbing across the centuries has allowed

66
00:03:55,400 --> 00:04:00,300
us to take the art and technology of the craft to new levels.

67
00:04:00,400 --> 00:04:07,060
We've got fixtures to pamper us, and fixtures that conserve water.

68
00:04:07,160 --> 00:04:12,160
Some elaborate systems pump water to the tops of 100-story

69
00:04:12,260 --> 00:04:17,100
skyscrapers, while others convert salty seawater into drinking

70
00:04:17,200 --> 00:04:20,300
water.

71
00:04:20,301 --> 00:04:22,940
State-of-the-art plumbing is even snaking its way into

72
00:04:23,040 --> 00:04:25,300
America's growing number of new sports arenas.

73
00:04:28,300 --> 00:04:32,080
San Diego's Petco Park boasts an array of plumbing advances

74
00:04:32,180 --> 00:04:35,120
that meet all the needs of 42,000 baseball fans.

75
00:04:37,720 --> 00:04:41,380
The project contained over 200,000 feet of pipe,

76
00:04:41,480 --> 00:04:44,560
which is approximately 38-plus miles.

77
00:04:44,660 --> 00:04:49,820
There are approximately 30 point-of-use electric water

78
00:04:49,821 --> 00:04:54,820
approximately 520 toilets, 225 urinals,

79
00:04:54,920 --> 00:04:59,460
and approximately 450 lavatories.

80
00:04:59,560 --> 00:05:02,460
Engineers designed the park's vast plumbing network

81
00:05:02,560 --> 00:05:05,900
not with blueprints, but with computer-assisted drawings,

82
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:08,240
or CADs.

83
00:05:08,340 --> 00:05:11,600
The CAD drawing system that we use expedites

84
00:05:11,700 --> 00:05:13,140
the drawing process.

85
00:05:13,240 --> 00:05:15,840
Rather than using the traditional, what we call,

86
00:05:15,940 --> 00:05:19,580
stick drawing, he can pull that up on a CAD

87
00:05:19,581 --> 00:05:22,480
and he can see the background of all systems.

88
00:05:22,580 --> 00:05:26,580
He looks at that for coordination.

89
00:05:26,680 --> 00:05:29,820
It allows him to see where the walls are,

90
00:05:29,920 --> 00:05:33,320
where the structure is, confirming

91
00:05:33,420 --> 00:05:37,040
that we don't have a duck line and a water line

92
00:05:37,140 --> 00:05:39,140
or a sewer line in the same location.

93
00:05:41,940 --> 00:05:43,640
All the water that the stadium uses

94
00:05:43,740 --> 00:05:46,240
for everything from its fountain and concession stands

95
00:05:46,241 --> 00:05:49,480
to field irrigation and firefighting first passes

96
00:05:49,580 --> 00:05:52,580
through here, the heart of the plumbing system.

97
00:05:52,680 --> 00:05:55,880
We are in the domestic water booster pump room.

98
00:05:55,980 --> 00:05:59,580
This is where the incoming water comes from the city.

99
00:05:59,680 --> 00:06:05,000
The water is boosted in pressure with a triplex pump system.

100
00:06:05,100 --> 00:06:09,400
This is to compensate for any variables in city water pressure

101
00:06:09,500 --> 00:06:12,400
to keep a constant water flow to the building itself.

102
00:06:12,401 --> 00:06:17,400
itself. Huge pumps also regulate

103
00:06:17,401 --> 00:06:20,400
water flow once it's in the stadium complex.

104
00:06:20,401 --> 00:06:23,400
This ensures that even during the seventh inning stretch, when

105
00:06:23,401 --> 00:06:27,400
40,000 fans just might use the bathrooms all at once, water

106
00:06:27,401 --> 00:06:32,400
pressure remains uninterrupted. When there's a demand for

107
00:06:32,401 --> 00:06:35,400
the water upstairs from the toilets being flushed and the

108
00:06:35,401 --> 00:06:40,400
sinks being used, this system will sense that and turn on the

109
00:06:40,401 --> 00:06:43,800
first pump, but not at the full operating level.

110
00:06:43,880 --> 00:06:47,340
This box is a computerized system inside.

111
00:06:47,400 --> 00:06:51,280
It actually has a little CPU inside that has sensors

112
00:06:51,340 --> 00:06:55,920
throughout this system to make it operate.

113
00:06:55,980 --> 00:06:58,980
Before the stadium opened, plumbing contractors

114
00:06:59,060 --> 00:07:02,180
tested this high-tech system using a method any fraternity

115
00:07:02,260 --> 00:07:05,660
prankster would salute.

116
00:07:05,720 --> 00:07:08,320
Hundreds of volunteers stationed themselves throughout

117
00:07:08,321 --> 00:07:12,520
the stadium's restrooms for a super flush.

118
00:07:12,620 --> 00:07:15,760
At a signal, they would simultaneously flush almost all

119
00:07:15,860 --> 00:07:23,440
of the 520 toilets to simulate the moment of maximum water

120
00:07:23,540 --> 00:07:25,840
usage during a game.

121
00:07:25,940 --> 00:07:27,600
The reason for the super flush is

122
00:07:27,700 --> 00:07:31,240
to make sure that the plumbing system works per design.

123
00:07:31,340 --> 00:07:34,880
And what we're talking about is to make sure that when we have

124
00:07:34,980 --> 00:07:38,320
a seventh inning stretch, when most people use it,

125
00:07:38,321 --> 00:07:40,720
the restrooms and go use the concession stands.

126
00:07:40,820 --> 00:07:44,560
The water usage is usually at its peak volume.

127
00:07:44,660 --> 00:07:48,500
And they're reassuring that the pipe sizes and the sewerage

128
00:07:48,600 --> 00:07:50,340
system will operate properly.

129
00:07:50,440 --> 00:07:52,640
And it actually went very well.

130
00:07:52,740 --> 00:07:55,740
And we had no backups, no overflows,

131
00:07:55,840 --> 00:07:59,140
and it was over quite quickly.

132
00:07:59,240 --> 00:08:01,980
The only thing more reliant on Petco's plumbing system

133
00:08:02,080 --> 00:08:07,020
than the fan's is its lush manicured turf.

134
00:08:07,021 --> 00:08:11,300
85 powerful sprinklers divided into 19 watering zones

135
00:08:11,301 --> 00:08:14,940
spray the 110,000 square foot playing field.

136
00:08:16,020 --> 00:08:20,760
On the average, this head puts out approximately 17 gallons per minute.

137
00:08:21,080 --> 00:08:23,600
So if we're running the system on an average night,

138
00:08:23,880 --> 00:08:26,500
one head would put out approximately 340 gallons.

139
00:08:27,060 --> 00:08:29,340
If you do the multiplication to get the whole field,

140
00:08:29,840 --> 00:08:31,860
on your average night during the season,

141
00:08:32,360 --> 00:08:35,660
if we were to water, it would be about 27,000 gallons of water on the whole field.

142
00:08:37,020 --> 00:08:40,120
Each sprinkler head is equipped with an electronic sensor

143
00:08:40,220 --> 00:08:44,720
and can be adjusted via the internet or by remote control.

144
00:08:44,820 --> 00:08:46,020
You can adjust the spray.

145
00:08:46,120 --> 00:08:48,760
You can adjust the nozzles, how far the spray is going,

146
00:08:48,860 --> 00:08:49,760
the degree.

147
00:08:49,860 --> 00:08:52,060
If you want to make it 180 degree or 230 degree,

148
00:08:52,160 --> 00:08:53,980
you know, baseball field has a lot of angles.

149
00:08:54,080 --> 00:08:56,080
You can be out there on the field with the remote

150
00:08:56,180 --> 00:08:58,780
and pop those heads up and make adjustments on the spot

151
00:08:58,880 --> 00:09:02,280
instead of running back and forth to the clock.

152
00:09:02,380 --> 00:09:03,920
Whether the water at Petco Park

153
00:09:03,921 --> 00:09:05,920
to the field or to the fans.

154
00:09:06,020 --> 00:09:09,020
All of it travels through pipes tailor-made to withstand

155
00:09:09,120 --> 00:09:11,920
California's ever-present earthquakes.

156
00:09:12,020 --> 00:09:15,900
If we look up above here, the overhead piping at this point

157
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:19,460
at a seismic joint requires some flexibility.

158
00:09:19,560 --> 00:09:23,200
And as you can see, you see the braided flex,

159
00:09:23,300 --> 00:09:26,100
and you'll see the additional fittings that are there.

160
00:09:26,200 --> 00:09:29,880
And in an earthquake, this allows that pipe to move

161
00:09:29,980 --> 00:09:33,920
and not twist, break.

162
00:09:33,921 --> 00:09:36,560
Most of us take high-tech plumbing systems like the one

163
00:09:36,660 --> 00:09:40,460
at Petco Park for granted, just as we give little thought

164
00:09:40,560 --> 00:09:43,660
to plumbing in our own homes.

165
00:09:43,760 --> 00:09:45,460
But surprisingly, most of America

166
00:09:45,560 --> 00:09:48,400
didn't have what we'd consider basic indoor plumbing

167
00:09:48,500 --> 00:09:52,380
until as recently as the turn of the 20th century.

168
00:09:52,480 --> 00:09:56,880
Still, its principles can be traced back thousands of years.

169
00:09:56,980 --> 00:09:58,920
All the early civilizations knew

170
00:09:59,020 --> 00:10:01,480
that they would take the water upstream

171
00:10:01,481 --> 00:10:04,880
and they discharged their waste downstream.

172
00:10:04,980 --> 00:10:06,480
If you look at the original plumbing,

173
00:10:06,580 --> 00:10:08,880
that was the premise for it.

174
00:10:08,980 --> 00:10:11,720
Archaeologists have discovered plumbing's earliest artifacts

175
00:10:11,820 --> 00:10:16,020
in Iraq, once the heart of ancient Mesopotamia.

176
00:10:16,120 --> 00:10:19,940
They've unearthed traces of a crude waste disposal system,

177
00:10:20,040 --> 00:10:26,440
primitive drains and cesspits dating back as far as 6000 BC.

178
00:10:26,540 --> 00:10:29,540
But excavators found no evidence of any method of transporting

179
00:10:29,541 --> 00:10:31,540
water to where the residents lived.

180
00:10:35,440 --> 00:10:38,840
1,500 miles away, in the Indus Valley,

181
00:10:38,940 --> 00:10:41,280
an ancient culture called the Harappins

182
00:10:41,380 --> 00:10:45,080
developed an impressive waste disposal system around 2500 BC.

183
00:10:48,400 --> 00:10:51,500
It began with something resembling a toilet.

184
00:10:51,600 --> 00:10:54,740
These structures were built of stacked brick with a seat

185
00:10:54,840 --> 00:10:58,500
and actually a wooden cover that went over the seat.

186
00:10:58,501 --> 00:11:02,680
And these then drained into, at intersections of streets,

187
00:11:02,780 --> 00:11:07,100
into cesspools, which had stairs so that the workers could walk

188
00:11:07,200 --> 00:11:11,120
down in and clean up.

189
00:11:11,220 --> 00:11:13,180
By the sixth century BC, builders

190
00:11:13,280 --> 00:11:15,520
of the Mesopotamian city of Babylon

191
00:11:15,620 --> 00:11:19,520
still hadn't solved the other half of plumbing's equation,

192
00:11:19,620 --> 00:11:24,020
a continuous freshwater delivery system.

193
00:11:24,120 --> 00:11:26,720
But their method of irrigating Babylon's famous hanging

194
00:11:26,721 --> 00:11:30,220
gardens, 350 feet atop the palace walls,

195
00:11:30,320 --> 00:11:31,720
was a step in the right direction.

196
00:11:35,020 --> 00:11:37,200
At the base of the palace wall, the Babylonians

197
00:11:37,300 --> 00:11:40,440
built a reservoir. The water was raised by means

198
00:11:40,540 --> 00:11:44,500
of a slave-powered chain pump, which had two large wheels,

199
00:11:44,600 --> 00:11:49,140
like a ski lift. Ceramic pots or buckets

200
00:11:49,240 --> 00:11:51,420
hanging from the chain were continuously dipped

201
00:11:51,520 --> 00:11:54,380
into the reservoir. Once water reached the top

202
00:11:54,381 --> 00:11:56,780
the gardens, it could be released from gates into

203
00:11:56,880 --> 00:11:58,080
irrigation troughs.

204
00:12:02,080 --> 00:12:06,120
By around 1500 BC, 800 miles from Babylon,

205
00:12:06,220 --> 00:12:09,620
the ancient Egyptians took water delivery a step further,

206
00:12:09,720 --> 00:12:14,440
diverting it inland from the Nile for irrigation and bathing.

207
00:12:14,540 --> 00:12:18,240
What they had were their artisans that were making

208
00:12:18,340 --> 00:12:22,540
their clay jugs figured out a way to, instead of making a jug

209
00:12:22,541 --> 00:12:25,040
with the bottom to it, they made it into pipe,

210
00:12:25,100 --> 00:12:26,980
and they made short sections.

211
00:12:27,040 --> 00:12:30,580
So the Egyptians were considered some of the first

212
00:12:30,640 --> 00:12:34,960
to be able to transport water through a conduit, through pipe.

213
00:12:37,520 --> 00:12:40,700
Around 400 BC, the ancient Greeks

214
00:12:40,760 --> 00:12:43,160
took plumbing to a whole new level

215
00:12:43,220 --> 00:12:45,300
by combining the use of waste removal

216
00:12:45,360 --> 00:12:47,160
and water transportation devices.

217
00:12:50,160 --> 00:12:52,540
To deliver water, the Greeks built

218
00:12:52,541 --> 00:12:56,140
aqueducts, stone-covered channels that followed the slope

219
00:12:56,141 --> 00:13:00,540
of the ground and let gravity do the work.

220
00:13:00,541 --> 00:13:03,780
To prevent enemies from sabotaging their water supply, the

221
00:13:03,781 --> 00:13:07,780
Greeks buried most aqueducts underground, to a depth of up to

222
00:13:07,781 --> 00:13:10,900
14 feet. Once the water flowed into the

223
00:13:10,901 --> 00:13:15,300
city, it was held in storage cisterns.

224
00:13:15,301 --> 00:13:17,940
The water was then used for fountains and the ancient world's

225
00:13:17,941 --> 00:13:21,280
the ancient world's newest plumbing innovation, the bathtub.

226
00:13:24,280 --> 00:13:27,080
To dispose of waste, many Greeks equipped their homes

227
00:13:27,081 --> 00:13:30,280
with latrines that drained into a sewer beneath the street.

228
00:13:33,620 --> 00:13:37,120
The sewer then carried the waste to surrounding rivers.

229
00:13:37,121 --> 00:13:39,260
The interesting thing is all these areas

230
00:13:39,261 --> 00:13:41,960
were still discharging to a water course.

231
00:13:41,961 --> 00:13:44,560
So they were, they didn't know that much about pollution,

232
00:13:44,561 --> 00:13:47,800
the fact that the human waste had an odor to it.

233
00:13:51,300 --> 00:13:54,540
As impressive as these early accomplishments were,

234
00:13:54,560 --> 00:13:57,940
they would soon be eclipsed by the greatest society of plumbers

235
00:13:57,980 --> 00:14:01,240
in history, the Romans.

236
00:14:09,020 --> 00:14:11,080
The California Aqueduct.

237
00:14:11,081 --> 00:14:14,380
Built in the 1970s, this man-made river is the largest

238
00:14:14,381 --> 00:14:17,380
in a system of aqueducts that supply the Los Angeles area

239
00:14:17,381 --> 00:14:25,320
with water from the north. And at 444 miles, it's the

240
00:14:25,321 --> 00:14:31,840
longest in the world. The prototype of this giant

241
00:14:31,841 --> 00:14:35,880
aqueduct was designed over 2,000 years ago by Roman

242
00:14:35,881 --> 00:14:40,840
engineers to deliver water to the capital of the United States.

243
00:14:40,841 --> 00:14:46,440
of their mighty empire. The Roman aqueduct was a

244
00:14:46,441 --> 00:14:51,960
milestone in antiquity's march toward effective plumbing.

245
00:14:51,961 --> 00:14:54,680
For inspiration, the Romans looked to their cultural and

246
00:14:54,681 --> 00:14:59,560
geographical neighbors, the Greeks.

247
00:14:59,561 --> 00:15:03,000
They borrowed the Greek concept of the aqueduct and supersized

248
00:15:03,001 --> 00:15:05,400
it. The Romans are known for

249
00:15:05,401 --> 00:15:08,040
their aqueduct system, not because they were the first,

250
00:15:08,041 --> 00:15:10,880
but because they transported the water the furthest.

251
00:15:10,980 --> 00:15:14,080
Their longest aqueduct was in the neighborhood of 57 miles

252
00:15:14,180 --> 00:15:15,040
long.

253
00:15:15,140 --> 00:15:17,420
They were taking it from the mountains down

254
00:15:17,520 --> 00:15:18,920
through their piping system.

255
00:15:19,020 --> 00:15:21,880
Most of their aqueduct system was underground pipe.

256
00:15:21,980 --> 00:15:24,080
It was only when they were stretching across the valleys

257
00:15:24,180 --> 00:15:29,500
where they had the elevated, what we know today as aqueducts.

258
00:15:29,600 --> 00:15:31,100
Once the water reached Rome,

259
00:15:31,200 --> 00:15:33,440
it was distributed through a grid of supply lines

260
00:15:33,540 --> 00:15:34,500
under the streets.

261
00:15:34,501 --> 00:15:39,580
The Romans built these miles of pipe with a new material they

262
00:15:39,581 --> 00:15:42,140
found on their exploits abroad.

263
00:15:42,141 --> 00:15:45,580
They had discovered lead in England

264
00:15:45,581 --> 00:15:49,120
and brought the lead back, and they started forming pipes

265
00:15:49,121 --> 00:15:53,120
and fixtures out of lead because it was soft and malleable.

266
00:15:53,121 --> 00:15:57,020
Of course, the word plumber or plumbing comes from the Latin

267
00:15:57,021 --> 00:15:59,860
word for lead, which was plumbium.

268
00:15:59,861 --> 00:16:04,860
The use of lead was such a marvelous plumbing and engineering

269
00:16:04,960 --> 00:16:05,860
feat.

270
00:16:05,960 --> 00:16:08,260
That really modernized plumbing more

271
00:16:08,360 --> 00:16:11,400
than what the Greeks had.

272
00:16:11,500 --> 00:16:13,500
The empire's lead pipes supplied

273
00:16:13,600 --> 00:16:15,840
the running water that was the lifeblood of one of Rome's

274
00:16:15,940 --> 00:16:19,980
most treasured institutions, the baths.

275
00:16:20,080 --> 00:16:22,520
According to legend, they spent approximately one third

276
00:16:22,620 --> 00:16:26,280
of their tax dollars on creating bath systems.

277
00:16:26,380 --> 00:16:28,680
They were very big into cleanliness.

278
00:16:28,681 --> 00:16:33,320
In early Roman times, the number of baths in Rome

279
00:16:33,420 --> 00:16:37,860
could bathe up to 50,000 people at one time.

280
00:16:37,960 --> 00:16:40,260
The emperors themselves used these baths

281
00:16:40,360 --> 00:16:44,300
as a means of giving the people a kind of wonderful

282
00:16:44,400 --> 00:16:47,940
entertainment facility, which would prevent them

283
00:16:48,040 --> 00:16:50,340
from thinking of other things like political freedom,

284
00:16:50,440 --> 00:16:52,340
for example.

285
00:16:52,440 --> 00:16:55,300
Beneath the splendor of the baths in lower chambers

286
00:16:55,400 --> 00:16:58,680
lay a marvel of engineering.

287
00:16:58,681 --> 00:17:02,380
Millions of gallons of water were heated by wood and coal

288
00:17:02,480 --> 00:17:05,320
fired furnaces to generate radiant heat in the levels

289
00:17:05,420 --> 00:17:06,120
above.

290
00:17:08,620 --> 00:17:11,220
Wastewater drained out of the baths and other buildings

291
00:17:11,320 --> 00:17:14,620
into large sewer pipes that emptied into the Tiber River.

292
00:17:16,840 --> 00:17:19,660
For the Romans, doing their business was often a social

293
00:17:19,760 --> 00:17:21,040
event.

294
00:17:21,140 --> 00:17:25,100
Public latrines had as many as 20 seats arranged so users could

295
00:17:25,200 --> 00:17:26,980
face each other.

296
00:17:26,981 --> 00:17:30,220
Water ran constantly beneath the seats, washing the waste

297
00:17:30,320 --> 00:17:32,020
into the nearest sewer.

298
00:17:32,120 --> 00:17:36,720
Showers, baths, using a bathroom, everybody, group

299
00:17:36,820 --> 00:17:39,220
activity was considered completely acceptable, quite

300
00:17:39,320 --> 00:17:40,220
normal.

301
00:17:40,320 --> 00:17:42,220
And in fact, anything to the contrary would have been

302
00:17:42,320 --> 00:17:43,740
regarded as odd by them.

303
00:17:43,840 --> 00:17:46,940
Not by us, but by them.

304
00:17:47,040 --> 00:17:51,040
When Rome finally fell in the fourth century AD, so too

305
00:17:51,140 --> 00:17:53,880
did antiquities' state-of-the-art plumbing.

306
00:17:53,980 --> 00:17:56,980
For once, Rome was conquered.

307
00:17:56,981 --> 00:18:00,820
not by a new civilization that surpassed it,

308
00:18:00,821 --> 00:18:03,220
but by a bunch of barbarian hordes

309
00:18:03,221 --> 00:18:05,980
who were basically nomads and plunderers.

310
00:18:05,981 --> 00:18:10,360
And they didn't really give two hoots about running water.

311
00:18:10,361 --> 00:18:12,220
They were never in one place long enough

312
00:18:12,221 --> 00:18:14,560
to enjoy indoor plumbing.

313
00:18:14,561 --> 00:18:17,440
At the same time, Christianity was taking over.

314
00:18:17,441 --> 00:18:20,340
And of course, Christianity was concerned

315
00:18:20,341 --> 00:18:22,700
with the decadence of the Roman baths.

316
00:18:22,701 --> 00:18:25,600
So there were a number of the religious leaders

317
00:18:25,601 --> 00:18:29,340
that basically said that bathing was forbidden.

318
00:18:29,440 --> 00:18:30,940
Medieval villagers reverted back

319
00:18:31,040 --> 00:18:34,940
to the simple cesspit, a hole dug in the ground where everyone

320
00:18:35,040 --> 00:18:39,840
dumped their waste. For centuries to come,

321
00:18:39,940 --> 00:18:42,280
cesspits were society's standard method of waste

322
00:18:42,380 --> 00:18:45,560
disposal. But they frequently overflowed,

323
00:18:45,660 --> 00:18:49,920
polluting nearby wells. Medieval innovators

324
00:18:50,020 --> 00:18:53,520
developed a new system that was no more sanitary, but a little

325
00:18:53,521 --> 00:18:55,560
private.

326
00:18:55,660 --> 00:18:58,720
In monasteries and castles, clergymen and counts used

327
00:18:58,820 --> 00:19:02,400
indoor toilets known as garter robes.

328
00:19:02,500 --> 00:19:05,400
The garter robe was a projection from a building, which

329
00:19:05,500 --> 00:19:08,840
inside contained a seat on which you sat and simply went straight

330
00:19:08,940 --> 00:19:11,740
down into the moat or the river or the ground below.

331
00:19:11,840 --> 00:19:14,280
And there are several surviving ones in England.

332
00:19:14,380 --> 00:19:17,580
And miserable menials would come and scrape away the dirt in

333
00:19:17,680 --> 00:19:20,580
the morning in grand establishments.

334
00:19:20,581 --> 00:19:24,420
City dwellers far from any moat had to rough it by using

335
00:19:24,520 --> 00:19:27,120
the less discreet chamber pot.

336
00:19:27,220 --> 00:19:29,380
If you were wealthy, a chamber pot

337
00:19:29,480 --> 00:19:33,520
would be an ornate ceramic bowl big enough for you

338
00:19:33,620 --> 00:19:35,620
to squat over.

339
00:19:35,720 --> 00:19:38,160
When the chamber pot was used, it

340
00:19:38,260 --> 00:19:40,060
was thrown out into the gutter.

341
00:19:40,160 --> 00:19:44,940
So there was really no sanitation at all.

342
00:19:45,040 --> 00:19:47,040
People began crowding into cities

343
00:19:47,140 --> 00:19:50,240
in the Middle Ages, and these unsanitary conditions

344
00:19:50,241 --> 00:19:53,680
triggered waves of deadly epidemics.

345
00:19:53,780 --> 00:19:56,080
Everybody knows about the Blubonic Plague

346
00:19:56,180 --> 00:19:59,020
or the Black Plague, but there were a number

347
00:19:59,120 --> 00:20:00,880
of other smaller plagues that took place

348
00:20:00,980 --> 00:20:06,420
because dysentery and typhoid were rampant throughout Europe.

349
00:20:06,520 --> 00:20:10,360
And one of the reasons for that was the unsanitary conditions.

350
00:20:10,460 --> 00:20:12,760
They knew that there was a mal odor

351
00:20:12,860 --> 00:20:14,360
and it was terrible and it smelled,

352
00:20:14,460 --> 00:20:18,740
but they didn't know that it was creating the disease.

353
00:20:18,741 --> 00:20:22,440
By the end of the 15th century, Europeans began to suspect

354
00:20:22,540 --> 00:20:24,880
that there might be a link between their crude waste

355
00:20:24,980 --> 00:20:29,120
disposal methods and the diseases that plagued them.

356
00:20:29,220 --> 00:20:33,120
So it was at the end of what we say during the Renaissance

357
00:20:33,220 --> 00:20:36,600
era that we started to see the revitalization of plumbing

358
00:20:36,700 --> 00:20:41,660
because of the starting to understand germs and bacteria.

359
00:20:44,240 --> 00:20:47,260
Europeans who could afford it now had servants cart

360
00:20:47,261 --> 00:20:50,860
their waste away. One enterprising Englishman went

361
00:20:50,861 --> 00:20:54,360
a step further. John Harrington, the godson of

362
00:20:54,361 --> 00:20:58,400
Queen Elizabeth I, recaptured antiquity's concept by devising

363
00:20:58,401 --> 00:21:04,320
a way to let water do the work. His invention marks the origin of

364
00:21:04,321 --> 00:21:10,520
the modern toilet, a.k.a. the John.

365
00:21:10,521 --> 00:21:14,520
It was a very primitive type, and he put it in Queen

366
00:21:14,521 --> 00:21:19,220
Elizabeth's one castle, and it was one of the first functioning

367
00:21:19,221 --> 00:21:24,100
water closets that they had water in it, and it would flush.

368
00:21:24,101 --> 00:21:27,560
Harrington's contraption had a tank filled with water and a

369
00:21:27,561 --> 00:21:30,600
valve that released the water so it could flush away the contents

370
00:21:30,601 --> 00:21:34,200
into a cesspit. But it was missing a crucial

371
00:21:34,201 --> 00:21:38,240
third element, a way to prevent the putrid cesspit gases from

372
00:21:38,241 --> 00:21:42,480
wafting back up through the toilet.

373
00:21:42,481 --> 00:21:45,780
Ridiculed by his peers, Harrington produced just two

374
00:21:45,880 --> 00:21:49,780
of his toilets. Almost two centuries would pass

375
00:21:49,880 --> 00:21:52,520
before a new generation of innovators would take up where

376
00:21:52,620 --> 00:21:56,220
Harrington left off and transform his invention into a

377
00:21:56,320 --> 00:21:58,020
household word.

378
00:22:07,140 --> 00:22:09,580
Few of us give much thought to the plumbing networks in our

379
00:22:09,581 --> 00:22:12,780
in our own homes. They run unnoticed,

380
00:22:12,880 --> 00:22:17,080
hidden behind living room walls and buried under foundations.

381
00:22:17,180 --> 00:22:20,120
We pour a glass of water, do a load of laundry,

382
00:22:20,220 --> 00:22:23,320
take a shower, all without thinking about how the water

383
00:22:23,420 --> 00:22:27,140
comes and goes. But the plumbing pipes

384
00:22:27,240 --> 00:22:29,060
are the essential veins and arteries

385
00:22:29,160 --> 00:22:33,400
that make a house a home. The cost of a home,

386
00:22:33,500 --> 00:22:37,080
about 17% of it is the cost of your plumbing system.

387
00:22:37,180 --> 00:22:38,980
So that puts it into perspective.

388
00:22:38,981 --> 00:22:42,420
there's quite a bit of amount of pipe.

389
00:22:42,520 --> 00:22:45,080
Although American houses come in a variety of shapes

390
00:22:45,180 --> 00:22:47,660
and sizes, the plumbing system in each of them

391
00:22:47,760 --> 00:22:52,320
has the same basic elements, pipes for incoming clean water,

392
00:22:52,420 --> 00:22:55,660
pipes for outgoing waste water, and pipes for venting air.

393
00:22:58,840 --> 00:23:01,200
Every system is installed in phases,

394
00:23:01,300 --> 00:23:05,600
beginning when the foundation of the house is still exposed.

395
00:23:05,700 --> 00:23:08,500
First, workers piece together the primary arteries

396
00:23:08,501 --> 00:23:13,100
water and drain pipes to the sewer.

397
00:23:13,200 --> 00:23:15,140
After workers erect the frame of the house,

398
00:23:15,240 --> 00:23:18,480
plumbers begin installing the plastic PVC pipes that will

399
00:23:18,580 --> 00:23:21,480
carry wastewater out of the home and into the sewer drain

400
00:23:21,580 --> 00:23:22,580
pipes.

401
00:23:24,520 --> 00:23:27,620
PVC piping is also used for venting.

402
00:23:27,720 --> 00:23:30,680
Each plumbing fixture is linked to a vent pipe.

403
00:23:30,780 --> 00:23:34,660
Venting allows foul-smelling sewer gas to escape while ensuring

404
00:23:34,760 --> 00:23:36,820
smooth water flow.

405
00:23:36,821 --> 00:23:40,420
The purpose of a vent is to have atmospheric pressure

406
00:23:40,421 --> 00:23:44,120
equally inside the plumbing system as we have on the outside,

407
00:23:44,121 --> 00:23:45,860
so we always have a free flow.

408
00:23:45,861 --> 00:23:49,660
A perfect example would be drawing up a liquid into a straw,

409
00:23:49,661 --> 00:23:53,060
putting your finger over it, the liquid will remain in there.

410
00:23:53,061 --> 00:23:54,580
That's a closed system.

411
00:23:54,581 --> 00:23:57,980
Take your finger off, that opens it up, allows free flow,

412
00:23:57,981 --> 00:24:02,080
and that liquid will flow out very easily.

413
00:24:02,081 --> 00:24:03,520
After the vent pipes are in place,

414
00:24:03,521 --> 00:24:05,220
plumbers run copper piping.

415
00:24:05,221 --> 00:24:07,960
piping to each fixture that requires an incoming supply

416
00:24:08,060 --> 00:24:13,060
of potable water. Over time, stronger copper pipes

417
00:24:13,160 --> 00:24:16,100
will withstand the pressure of incoming water better than PVC

418
00:24:16,200 --> 00:24:19,700
pipes. Separate lines are run for hot

419
00:24:19,800 --> 00:24:24,200
and cold water. Unlike PVC pipes, which can be

420
00:24:24,300 --> 00:24:28,840
glued together, copper pipes must be soldered.

421
00:24:28,940 --> 00:24:32,240
After all these pipes are installed, the primary or rough

422
00:24:32,241 --> 00:24:37,540
plumbing is finished. Once this has been inspected,

423
00:24:37,541 --> 00:24:42,480
then drywall goes over this. It becomes painted, and the end user,

424
00:24:42,481 --> 00:24:45,480
the homeowner, they never really get to see what's behind the wall,

425
00:24:45,481 --> 00:24:47,680
and eventually they hang a picture of grandma up here,

426
00:24:47,681 --> 00:24:50,900
and everybody smiles. But behind it, that's their plumbing pipes

427
00:24:50,901 --> 00:24:54,000
going up to feed the hot and cold water up there to the master bath

428
00:24:54,001 --> 00:25:01,140
on the second floor. 300 years ago, in colonial

429
00:25:01,141 --> 00:25:04,380
America, the only pipes and houses were the kinds that men

430
00:25:04,381 --> 00:25:08,140
stuffed with tobacco. Colonists didn't think too

431
00:25:08,141 --> 00:25:10,780
much about indoor plumbing, mainly because they didn't see

432
00:25:10,781 --> 00:25:13,880
a need for it. Cleanliness was not a

433
00:25:13,881 --> 00:25:17,200
treasured value of colonial America, by and large.

434
00:25:17,201 --> 00:25:20,900
People rarely bathe. They rarely took baths.

435
00:25:20,901 --> 00:25:27,000
They might do so once a month, a couple of times a year.

436
00:25:27,001 --> 00:25:30,480
Even so, as the colonists built their cities, running water

437
00:25:30,481 --> 00:25:34,480
priority, not for bathing, but for firefighting.

438
00:25:34,481 --> 00:25:38,480
In the mid-1600s, workers in Boston built America's first

439
00:25:38,481 --> 00:25:42,480
water system to supply firefighters.

440
00:25:42,481 --> 00:25:46,480
They joined hollow logs together to create a water main.

441
00:25:46,481 --> 00:25:49,480
To access the water, firemen would simply drill through the

442
00:25:49,481 --> 00:25:52,480
walls of these primitive pipes and plug the hole when they

443
00:25:52,481 --> 00:25:57,480
were finished. When it came to waste disposal,

444
00:25:57,481 --> 00:26:00,480
the great outdoors proved an appealing alternative to the

445
00:26:00,520 --> 00:26:03,980
old country's chamber pots.

446
00:26:04,020 --> 00:26:07,480
But before long, Yankee ingenuity gave birth to an

447
00:26:07,520 --> 00:26:13,160
American institution, the outhouse.

448
00:26:13,200 --> 00:26:17,160
Outhouses were basically the bathrooms of the early

449
00:26:17,200 --> 00:26:20,840
colonial settlers in this country, and some of them were

450
00:26:20,880 --> 00:26:24,280
quite ornate. The technology was

451
00:26:24,300 --> 00:26:26,680
simple enough. A deep hole in the earth,

452
00:26:26,681 --> 00:26:30,280
a seat and a wooden shed with a door.

453
00:26:30,380 --> 00:26:32,980
Outhouses continued to proliferate throughout the 19th

454
00:26:33,080 --> 00:26:36,620
century in America, well beyond the advent of the indoor toilet.

455
00:26:36,720 --> 00:26:39,620
Present!

456
00:26:39,720 --> 00:26:41,620
With independence came innovation.

457
00:26:41,720 --> 00:26:46,740
And by the mid-1800s, Americans began to bring plumbing

458
00:26:46,840 --> 00:26:48,340
indoors.

459
00:26:48,440 --> 00:26:53,280
The homeowner would build a cistern and place it in the

460
00:26:53,380 --> 00:26:54,740
attic of the house.

461
00:26:54,741 --> 00:26:57,840
The water would be pumped up into that cistern from some

462
00:26:57,841 --> 00:26:59,280
external source.

463
00:26:59,281 --> 00:27:03,440
The water would flow by gravity from the cistern into a variety

464
00:27:03,441 --> 00:27:06,280
of devices.

465
00:27:06,281 --> 00:27:08,780
In the following decades, one of these devices

466
00:27:08,781 --> 00:27:11,900
would be an improved incarnation of John Harrington's breakthrough

467
00:27:11,901 --> 00:27:12,900
invention.

468
00:27:15,700 --> 00:27:18,440
Across the Atlantic, innovators in England began flooding

469
00:27:18,441 --> 00:27:21,400
British patent offices with updated versions of the water

470
00:27:21,401 --> 00:27:22,700
closet.

471
00:27:22,701 --> 00:27:28,000
Many models incorporated a new trap seal mechanism.

472
00:27:28,100 --> 00:27:30,640
In theory, the trap seal was an effective solution to the

473
00:27:30,740 --> 00:27:35,340
reeking odors that wafted up through Harrington's toilet.

474
00:27:35,440 --> 00:27:38,480
A U-shaped section of pipe beneath the drain and toilet

475
00:27:38,580 --> 00:27:42,360
bowl used a simple law of physics to create a water seal,

476
00:27:42,460 --> 00:27:45,960
blocking sewer gases from entering the home.

477
00:27:46,060 --> 00:27:48,860
But the trap seal by itself wasn't enough.

478
00:27:48,960 --> 00:27:50,760
The answer was venting.

479
00:27:50,761 --> 00:27:53,600
venting. What the vent does is allow air

480
00:27:53,601 --> 00:27:57,600
to come into the drainage system so when that water falls, the air

481
00:27:57,601 --> 00:28:01,600
goes with it, and it doesn't suck the water out of that trap.

482
00:28:01,601 --> 00:28:05,600
Very, very simple. In the late 19th century,

483
00:28:05,601 --> 00:28:08,600
with the principle of venting applied to the water closet, a

484
00:28:08,601 --> 00:28:13,600
revolutionary precursor to the modern toilet finally emerged.

485
00:28:13,601 --> 00:28:18,000
But identifying the inventor of the toilet is a bit tricky.

486
00:28:18,001 --> 00:28:21,600
The most controversial figure in the history of plumbing had a

487
00:28:21,601 --> 00:28:26,000
name as fitting as it was farcical, Thomas Crapper.

488
00:28:29,040 --> 00:28:32,840
When this biography of Crapper was published in 1969, many

489
00:28:32,841 --> 00:28:36,960
believed it was a hoax. Did Crapper actually invent the

490
00:28:36,961 --> 00:28:41,360
forerunner of the modern toilet? Did the man really exist?

491
00:28:41,361 --> 00:28:45,360
Even today, those who are privy to Crapper's story continue the

492
00:28:45,361 --> 00:28:48,000
debate. Originally,

493
00:28:48,001 --> 00:28:50,300
Originally, the thing that Crapper developed

494
00:28:50,301 --> 00:28:52,200
was called the water waste preventer.

495
00:28:52,201 --> 00:28:55,480
And if you lift the lid of your loo now,

496
00:28:55,481 --> 00:28:58,440
it's exactly the same as when he developed it 100 years ago.

497
00:28:58,441 --> 00:29:00,520
Americans cannot get over the fact

498
00:29:00,521 --> 00:29:03,020
that there was a man selling plumbing fittings

499
00:29:03,021 --> 00:29:04,780
with a name like Crapper.

500
00:29:04,781 --> 00:29:07,980
Now, that's amusing, and it's great, and it's fun.

501
00:29:07,981 --> 00:29:10,880
But that he invented the loo, and he

502
00:29:10,881 --> 00:29:13,500
had anything to do with inventing the loo,

503
00:29:13,501 --> 00:29:14,960
is, I'm afraid, a myth.

504
00:29:14,961 --> 00:29:17,320
James Watt didn't invent the steam engine.

505
00:29:17,321 --> 00:29:18,280
He just made it work.

506
00:29:18,860 --> 00:29:20,460
Marconi didn't invent wireless.

507
00:29:20,620 --> 00:29:22,240
He just made it practical.

508
00:29:22,380 --> 00:29:23,360
It's the same with Trapper.

509
00:29:24,120 --> 00:29:26,260
A lot of people were working on it at the time,

510
00:29:26,320 --> 00:29:27,820
and he made one that worked.

511
00:29:28,060 --> 00:29:31,320
The WC Pan, which is universally recognised today,

512
00:29:31,720 --> 00:29:35,920
was designed by Frederick Humpherson in 1884.

513
00:29:36,760 --> 00:29:38,700
That's my great-uncle, Frederick Humpherson,

514
00:29:38,701 --> 00:29:43,560
and this is the famous WC Pan, the Beaufort,

515
00:29:43,820 --> 00:29:46,700
the first pedestal wash-down closet.

516
00:29:47,320 --> 00:29:51,320
In the mid-1800s, as water closets were making a big splash

517
00:29:51,420 --> 00:29:54,320
in Western society, so was an unprecedented abundance

518
00:29:54,420 --> 00:29:55,820
of sewage.

519
00:29:55,920 --> 00:29:58,320
The cities of Europe were the first to pay the unseemly

520
00:29:58,420 --> 00:29:59,960
consequences.

521
00:30:00,060 --> 00:30:04,880
What finally happened is you had places like London where

522
00:30:04,980 --> 00:30:10,480
the Thames was so filled with sewage and the mal-odor, and

523
00:30:10,580 --> 00:30:14,080
Parliament meets right on the Thames River.

524
00:30:14,081 --> 00:30:16,980
So Parliament could not stand the odor.

525
00:30:17,080 --> 00:30:19,820
It was so bad that they could not meet.

526
00:30:19,920 --> 00:30:23,120
That's when they started to pass some of the bills to address

527
00:30:23,220 --> 00:30:27,820
the treatment of the sewage and try to transport it away from

528
00:30:27,920 --> 00:30:30,760
these main watercourses.

529
00:30:30,860 --> 00:30:34,760
In 1858, London's newly formed Metropolitan Board

530
00:30:34,860 --> 00:30:38,780
of Works undertook a historic project, the construction of

531
00:30:38,880 --> 00:30:42,000
London's first sewer system, designed to carry the waste

532
00:30:42,001 --> 00:30:46,240
away from the city. During the same decade in Chicago,

533
00:30:46,241 --> 00:30:50,740
workers built America's first integrated sewer system.

534
00:30:50,741 --> 00:30:54,080
But the young city was facing a contamination problem that sewers

535
00:30:54,081 --> 00:30:58,720
alone couldn't solve. Increasing quantities of raw

536
00:30:58,721 --> 00:31:02,320
sewage were being dumped into the Chicago River, the only

537
00:31:02,321 --> 00:31:07,520
viable conduit to carry away the enormous daily flow of waste.

538
00:31:07,521 --> 00:31:10,960
But the river emptied into Lake Michigan, the city's only source

539
00:31:10,961 --> 00:31:12,760
source of drinking water.

540
00:31:12,860 --> 00:31:15,440
As more and more people started coming into the city,

541
00:31:15,540 --> 00:31:17,800
they had to take those water intakes

542
00:31:17,900 --> 00:31:21,740
and move them further and further into the lake.

543
00:31:21,840 --> 00:31:24,980
And for a while, it worked.

544
00:31:25,080 --> 00:31:30,540
But then in 1885, there was a tremendous four-day rainstorm

545
00:31:30,640 --> 00:31:34,420
that just flushed all these primitive sewers way out

546
00:31:34,520 --> 00:31:37,620
into the lake so that the water intake cribs

547
00:31:37,720 --> 00:31:40,960
were inundated with this polluted material.

548
00:31:40,961 --> 00:31:44,660
of the entire population of the city of Chicago perished

549
00:31:44,760 --> 00:31:48,960
from waterborne diseases during that time, cholera, typhoid,

550
00:31:49,060 --> 00:31:51,040
et cetera, dysentery.

551
00:31:51,140 --> 00:31:55,040
Chicago now faced a nearly insurmountable problem.

552
00:31:55,140 --> 00:31:58,480
The answer was a mind-boggling feat of civil engineering.

553
00:31:58,580 --> 00:32:02,620
Workers constructed a 28-mile canal connecting the Chicago

554
00:32:02,720 --> 00:32:06,380
River to the Des Plaines River, a tributary of the Mississippi.

555
00:32:06,480 --> 00:32:09,120
This reversed the flow of the Chicago River.

556
00:32:09,121 --> 00:32:12,360
Its water now flowed away from Lake Michigan, preventing sewage

557
00:32:12,460 --> 00:32:13,560
from entering it.

558
00:32:17,460 --> 00:32:20,860
The ambitious project, successfully completed in 1900,

559
00:32:20,960 --> 00:32:24,360
would serve as an example for other American cities.

560
00:32:24,460 --> 00:32:27,740
They, too, were coping with the growing popularity of indoor

561
00:32:27,840 --> 00:32:31,780
plumbing and with ever-increasing amounts of sewage.

562
00:32:39,120 --> 00:32:42,960
A new generation of plumbing students with pipe dreams of

563
00:32:43,060 --> 00:32:46,660
becoming masters of the craft is clogging up the industry's

564
00:32:46,760 --> 00:32:48,360
training facilities.

565
00:32:48,460 --> 00:32:49,960
Push up on it, make sure you roll your...

566
00:32:50,060 --> 00:32:52,400
The local 501 training school in Aurora, Illinois,

567
00:32:52,500 --> 00:32:55,740
includes a mock-up of a three-story house, offering a

568
00:32:55,840 --> 00:32:58,980
hands-on education that some find a little draining.

569
00:33:01,540 --> 00:33:04,480
Each student must complete a five-year program, which

570
00:33:04,580 --> 00:33:07,040
includes an apprenticeship and a state exam.

571
00:33:07,140 --> 00:33:08,380
Here you go.

572
00:33:08,381 --> 00:33:13,380
The facility certifies up to 90 trainees as plumbers every year.

573
00:33:13,480 --> 00:33:15,380
I could make an installer out of anybody.

574
00:33:15,480 --> 00:33:18,020
They could install pipes and have no concept of why they do

575
00:33:18,120 --> 00:33:21,320
what they do or why it fails and why somebody would get sick,

576
00:33:21,420 --> 00:33:23,420
whereas in here we teach them the science.

577
00:33:23,520 --> 00:33:26,540
We teach them the physics, the math, and all the things,

578
00:33:26,640 --> 00:33:29,540
the mechanical aspects that go into the industry

579
00:33:29,640 --> 00:33:31,540
to make them a professional.

580
00:33:31,640 --> 00:33:35,040
If one message rings loud and clear with the students here,

581
00:33:35,140 --> 00:33:37,380
it's that the critical measure of any plumbing system

582
00:33:37,381 --> 00:33:40,880
system is its hygiene. They're trained to adhere to

583
00:33:40,881 --> 00:33:44,280
state and federal laws designed to provide people with both a

584
00:33:44,281 --> 00:33:47,280
safe water supply and an effective means of disposing

585
00:33:47,281 --> 00:33:52,420
waste. The origins of such safety

586
00:33:52,421 --> 00:33:55,540
standards date back to the US Civil War.

587
00:33:55,541 --> 00:33:59,540
A sanitation crisis arose during the conflict, as more soldiers

588
00:33:59,541 --> 00:34:02,740
were dying from waterborne diseases, like dysentery and

589
00:34:02,741 --> 00:34:06,740
typhoid, than from battle injuries.

590
00:34:06,741 --> 00:34:09,400
Later, government studies proved that such diseases

591
00:34:09,500 --> 00:34:12,920
could be traced to bacteria in contaminated water supplies

592
00:34:13,020 --> 00:34:16,120
and waste inadequately discarded.

593
00:34:16,220 --> 00:34:19,260
In the late 19th century, especially after about 1890,

594
00:34:19,360 --> 00:34:21,860
Americans finally accepted the germ theory.

595
00:34:21,960 --> 00:34:26,620
And the reason we have stark white fixtures made of porcelain

596
00:34:26,720 --> 00:34:31,800
now is because of this crusade to create a germ-free environment.

597
00:34:31,900 --> 00:34:35,100
The bathroom now became this almost

598
00:34:35,101 --> 00:34:39,180
hospital-like environment, completely tiled, no wood,

599
00:34:39,280 --> 00:34:42,140
all of the pipes exposed.

600
00:34:42,240 --> 00:34:45,340
By the 1920s, architects were designing

601
00:34:45,440 --> 00:34:48,180
such antiseptically conceived bathrooms into new homes

602
00:34:48,280 --> 00:34:51,680
as standard features, along with hot water heaters

603
00:34:51,780 --> 00:34:53,080
and fully plumbed kitchens.

604
00:34:57,320 --> 00:35:00,000
Bathtubs and toilets could hardly roll off assembly lines

605
00:35:00,100 --> 00:35:02,920
fast enough to satisfy the demand of hygiene-conscious

606
00:35:03,020 --> 00:35:04,100
consumers.

607
00:35:04,101 --> 00:35:06,440
Manufacturers helped feed the frenzy.

608
00:35:06,540 --> 00:35:10,680
Starting around 1920, an old industry magazine

609
00:35:10,780 --> 00:35:14,580
started publishing a campaign for a bath a day

610
00:35:14,680 --> 00:35:16,480
in American society.

611
00:35:16,580 --> 00:35:19,920
And over 100,000 pamphlets were distributed to schools

612
00:35:20,020 --> 00:35:22,560
and to plumbing customers throughout the United States,

613
00:35:22,660 --> 00:35:26,080
urging them to take a bath a day.

614
00:35:26,180 --> 00:35:29,120
The plumbing boom wasn't without setbacks, however.

615
00:35:29,220 --> 00:35:31,700
Substandard plumbing continued to trigger outbreaks

616
00:35:31,701 --> 00:35:34,440
deadly waterborne diseases.

617
00:35:34,540 --> 00:35:38,440
At the 1933 Chicago World's Fair, sewage from toilets

618
00:35:38,540 --> 00:35:41,040
backed up into the clean water supply,

619
00:35:41,140 --> 00:35:45,640
sparking a dysentery epidemic that killed 98 people.

620
00:35:45,740 --> 00:35:48,520
This and other seemingly avoidable tragedies

621
00:35:48,620 --> 00:35:51,220
compelled civic leaders to call for plumbing industry

622
00:35:51,320 --> 00:35:52,580
regulations.

623
00:35:52,680 --> 00:35:55,860
And cities began to pass plumbing ordinances

624
00:35:55,960 --> 00:35:59,700
dictating exactly how things could be attached in your house,

625
00:35:59,701 --> 00:36:02,600
exactly right down to the size of the pipe that you could use,

626
00:36:02,700 --> 00:36:05,000
exactly what kinds of fixtures you could use,

627
00:36:05,100 --> 00:36:08,440
how they should be installed, where they could be installed.

628
00:36:08,540 --> 00:36:12,440
The era of the professional plumber had arrived.

629
00:36:12,540 --> 00:36:15,680
Industry leaders formed professional associations

630
00:36:15,780 --> 00:36:20,260
and instituted formal apprenticeship programs.

631
00:36:20,360 --> 00:36:22,160
All the while, plumbing technology

632
00:36:22,260 --> 00:36:24,460
continued to advance.

633
00:36:24,560 --> 00:36:28,220
In the 1930s, innovators at the Watts Regulator Company

634
00:36:28,221 --> 00:36:32,620
devised a sorely needed gadget for America's water heaters.

635
00:36:32,720 --> 00:36:34,860
To this day, the company manufactures

636
00:36:34,960 --> 00:36:36,760
this essential component.

637
00:36:36,860 --> 00:36:39,960
It's known in the trade as a TNP valve.

638
00:36:40,060 --> 00:36:42,760
Without a temperature and pressure relief valve,

639
00:36:42,860 --> 00:36:44,940
the pressures inside of a water heater

640
00:36:45,040 --> 00:36:48,980
can build up to the extent that your water heater may someday

641
00:36:49,080 --> 00:36:53,780
take off and end up in a neighbor's house maybe a quarter

642
00:36:53,880 --> 00:36:54,980
mile away.

643
00:36:55,080 --> 00:36:58,220
These things used to happen quite commonly in America.

644
00:36:58,221 --> 00:37:02,000
They still happen once in a while in homes where the water

645
00:37:02,001 --> 00:37:03,760
heater does not have a T&P valve.

646
00:37:03,761 --> 00:37:07,560
But this was a great invention that I'm sure has saved untold

647
00:37:07,561 --> 00:37:08,660
lives in America.

648
00:37:08,661 --> 00:37:11,300
And most people don't even know they have them.

649
00:37:15,880 --> 00:37:19,640
By the 1950s, with the help of a few critical inventions

650
00:37:19,641 --> 00:37:23,520
and a lot of laws, America had overcome the hurdle of creating

651
00:37:23,521 --> 00:37:26,020
safe and sanitary plumbing for its citizens.

652
00:37:28,220 --> 00:37:30,560
The plumbing students in Aurora, Illinois,

653
00:37:30,660 --> 00:37:34,660
are learning the basics of preserving that standard.

654
00:37:34,760 --> 00:37:36,960
Part of their curriculum includes the opportunity

655
00:37:37,060 --> 00:37:39,960
to install the growing variety of plumbing fixtures in their

656
00:37:40,060 --> 00:37:42,960
unusual classrooms.

657
00:37:43,060 --> 00:37:46,380
We're standing in the mock residential house

658
00:37:46,480 --> 00:37:50,280
that we teach our residential plumbers how to install

659
00:37:50,380 --> 00:37:55,120
normal residential plumbing, waste and vents, water pipe,

660
00:37:55,220 --> 00:37:56,780
setting tubs, things like that.

661
00:37:56,781 --> 00:38:00,520
The basic stuff they're going to do every day out in the field.

662
00:38:00,620 --> 00:38:02,220
The school has another specialized

663
00:38:02,320 --> 00:38:06,220
classroom, the so-called wet lab.

664
00:38:06,320 --> 00:38:09,120
Here, students get up close and personal with the inner workings

665
00:38:09,220 --> 00:38:13,120
of all kinds of plumbing fixtures.

666
00:38:13,220 --> 00:38:16,740
One special display illustrates how water-conserving toilets

667
00:38:16,840 --> 00:38:21,240
can flush with the same gusto as the old 3.5 gallon models.

668
00:38:21,340 --> 00:38:25,080
What you'll see is that 1.6 gallons of water will take, say,

669
00:38:25,081 --> 00:38:27,420
a half dozen golf balls, and slowly convey them

670
00:38:27,520 --> 00:38:28,720
down this waistline.

671
00:38:28,820 --> 00:38:30,980
And we run it in four-inch clear plastic

672
00:38:31,080 --> 00:38:35,260
so that the apprentices can see both how pitch, as far as the

673
00:38:35,360 --> 00:38:37,560
slope of the pipe, and the quantity of water

674
00:38:37,660 --> 00:38:41,700
would carry solids down the drainage system.

675
00:38:41,800 --> 00:38:43,600
What's in plumbing's future?

676
00:38:43,700 --> 00:38:46,200
Some innovative fixtures are poised to shower us

677
00:38:46,300 --> 00:38:50,580
with new pleasures, while others place yet another remote control

678
00:38:50,680 --> 00:38:53,300
in the hands of the American male.

679
00:38:55,080 --> 00:38:59,080
The safe, sanitary plumbing in your house

680
00:38:59,180 --> 00:39:00,920
is one of those technological triumphs

681
00:39:01,020 --> 00:39:05,720
you never really appreciate until something goes wrong.

682
00:39:05,820 --> 00:39:07,960
When that happens, you can call one of the nation's

683
00:39:08,060 --> 00:39:10,320
300,000 plumbers.

684
00:39:10,420 --> 00:39:12,900
Our typical calls are throughout the day

685
00:39:13,000 --> 00:39:15,900
are generally your drain cleaning.

686
00:39:16,000 --> 00:39:19,400
But we also, we have about 50% of our calls

687
00:39:19,500 --> 00:39:23,000
are also repair plumbing, water heaters, sewer line replacements,

688
00:39:23,001 --> 00:39:26,300
replacements, slab leaks, drippy faucets, garbage

689
00:39:26,301 --> 00:39:29,100
disposals, that kind of thing.

690
00:39:29,101 --> 00:39:31,300
Today, plumber Don Hatcher's challenge

691
00:39:31,301 --> 00:39:36,120
is to replace some leaky pipes buried beneath a suburban yard.

692
00:39:36,121 --> 00:39:37,880
In the past, the conventional approach

693
00:39:37,881 --> 00:39:40,560
required a lot of destructive digging.

694
00:39:40,561 --> 00:39:44,120
But now there's a better way.

695
00:39:44,121 --> 00:39:47,320
So-called trenchless technology enables plumbers

696
00:39:47,321 --> 00:39:50,660
to toss aside their shovels and insert new pipes directly

697
00:39:50,661 --> 00:39:52,860
the old defective ones.

698
00:39:52,960 --> 00:39:54,900
What we're going to do is we're going to feed a cable

699
00:39:55,000 --> 00:39:58,900
through the line and actually pull a stainless steel

700
00:39:59,000 --> 00:40:01,300
bursting head through the line.

701
00:40:01,400 --> 00:40:04,300
And right directly behind that will be a new polyethylene

702
00:40:04,400 --> 00:40:06,300
pipe.

703
00:40:06,400 --> 00:40:09,020
Even as we shell out the dollars to keep our old plumbing

704
00:40:09,120 --> 00:40:11,920
systems working, we can also indulge in shopping

705
00:40:12,020 --> 00:40:15,320
for the industry's latest cutting edge fixtures.

706
00:40:15,420 --> 00:40:18,820
This has a waterfall shower head above.

707
00:40:18,821 --> 00:40:20,820
If you ever wanted to sit under a waterfall,

708
00:40:20,920 --> 00:40:23,400
this has the perfect sensation of doing that.

709
00:40:23,500 --> 00:40:26,600
The water is pumped out of the reservoir on the bottom here,

710
00:40:26,700 --> 00:40:28,560
up and out, either to the body sprays

711
00:40:28,660 --> 00:40:30,900
or to the waterfall there.

712
00:40:31,000 --> 00:40:33,860
This is the latest in toiletry technology.

713
00:40:33,960 --> 00:40:37,680
You can flush it remotely.

714
00:40:37,780 --> 00:40:41,040
You can raise the one seat.

715
00:40:41,140 --> 00:40:44,140
You can raise the second seat with the remote.

716
00:40:44,240 --> 00:40:46,640
And you can also control all the bidet functions

717
00:40:46,740 --> 00:40:48,820
with the remote as well.

718
00:40:48,821 --> 00:40:51,120
Once you're done, look at the day cycle,

719
00:40:51,220 --> 00:40:53,360
and you turn on the dryer that dries your bottom

720
00:40:53,460 --> 00:40:55,720
when you're still sitting there.

721
00:40:55,820 --> 00:40:57,720
While plumbing designers continue to dream up

722
00:40:57,820 --> 00:41:01,460
fashionable fixtures, they must also consider the profound issues

723
00:41:01,560 --> 00:41:04,360
facing the industry.

724
00:41:04,460 --> 00:41:06,280
The greatest, perhaps, is a concern shared

725
00:41:06,380 --> 00:41:11,980
by municipalities around the world, water conservation.

726
00:41:12,080 --> 00:41:15,520
Somebody such as myself, who may be discussing plumbing history

727
00:41:15,521 --> 00:41:24,820
25, 30, 40, 50 years from now may look back and define one of the watersheds of plumbing history

728
00:41:24,821 --> 00:41:32,080
as the 1992 Plumbing Products Efficiency Act, which involved the federal government mandating

729
00:41:32,081 --> 00:41:36,718
that no toilet will flush with more than 1.6 gallons of water.

730
00:41:36,719 --> 00:41:39,560
The previous standard was 3.5 gallons.

731
00:41:39,561 --> 00:41:44,095
They also put restrictions on flows of faucets and showerheads as well.

732
00:41:44,096 --> 00:41:45,500
I think it's the first

733
00:41:45,501 --> 00:41:49,740
of what is going to be a continuing trend towards making

734
00:41:49,840 --> 00:41:54,040
plumbing products more environmentally friendly.

735
00:41:54,140 --> 00:41:56,840
To meet the monumental challenge of conserving the world's

736
00:41:56,940 --> 00:41:59,540
precious water supply, the plumbing industry

737
00:41:59,640 --> 00:42:03,060
is taking bold new steps.

738
00:42:03,160 --> 00:42:05,560
As shocking as it sounds, scientists

739
00:42:05,660 --> 00:42:08,620
are conducting experiments to introduce recycled wastewater

740
00:42:08,720 --> 00:42:11,600
into potable water supplies.

741
00:42:11,700 --> 00:42:13,460
They can treat any kind of water.

742
00:42:13,461 --> 00:42:16,220
the dirtier it is, the more it costs, but you can treat it.

743
00:42:16,320 --> 00:42:19,160
And then they've also established processing

744
00:42:19,260 --> 00:42:22,000
treatments for water treatment, so that literally you could

745
00:42:22,100 --> 00:42:24,100
take this sewage water very shortly

746
00:42:24,200 --> 00:42:26,700
and use it for drinking water.

747
00:42:26,800 --> 00:42:29,100
If such technology prevails,

748
00:42:29,200 --> 00:42:33,520
the future may bring us full circle to the ancient past.

749
00:42:33,620 --> 00:42:36,020
As it was in the world of our ancestors,

750
00:42:36,120 --> 00:42:39,160
the distinction may blur between the water we drink

751
00:42:39,260 --> 00:42:43,460
and the water we use to discard our waste.

752
00:42:43,461 --> 00:42:45,460
What separates then from now, however,

753
00:42:45,560 --> 00:42:48,760
is advanced technology that makes such a reality safe

754
00:42:48,860 --> 00:42:50,860
for all of us.

755
00:42:50,960 --> 00:42:55,500
History has proven that plumbing, sanitation,

756
00:42:55,600 --> 00:42:58,900
both in waste removal and in potable water,

757
00:42:59,000 --> 00:43:01,520
is at the forefront of safety and health,

758
00:43:01,620 --> 00:43:04,240
and that there has been nothing greater, no medicine, nothing

759
00:43:04,340 --> 00:43:08,720
yet to date that protects us better than sanitation.

760
00:43:08,820 --> 00:43:10,020
Plumbing.

761
00:43:10,120 --> 00:43:12,860
In many ways, it's out of sight and out of mind,

762
00:43:12,861 --> 00:43:16,160
something we consider only when a sink backs up or a toilet

763
00:43:16,240 --> 00:43:19,860
overflows. But it's a marvel disguised

764
00:43:19,940 --> 00:43:25,980
as the mundane and a cornerstone of humanity's health.

